(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-case-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Case Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 26-year-old man had a ventricular septal defect closed with a prosthetic patch at age 4. Echocardiography now shows a small residual shunt at the edge of the patch. He needs a tooth extraction, has no allergies and takes pills. Which plan fits?","choices":["Oral azithromycin beforehand","Amoxicillin for 5 days after","No prophylaxis needed","Oral amoxicillin beforehand"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Repaired congenital heart disease with a residual shunt at or next to a prosthetic patch is a high-risk condition, and an extraction manipulates the gums. With no allergy and an oral route, he takes a single dose of amoxicillin 30-60 minutes before. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 35, 36, 42"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman who had rheumatic heart disease as a teenager and now has mitral regurgitation presents with a temperature of 38.3 °C (100.9 °F) and painful, red nodules on the pads of several fingertips. Blood cultures and echocardiography have not yet been done. What is her modified Duke classification at this point?","choices":["Not yet possible or definite","Possible: three minor criteria","Definite: two major criteria","Possible: one major, one minor"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rheumatic mitral regurgitation is a predisposing heart condition (valve disease with regurgitation), 38.3 °C meets the fever criterion of 38.0 °C or more, and painful finger-pad nodules (Osler nodes) are an immunologic phenomenon. Three minor criteria and no major make the case possible. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with native mitral valve endocarditis was started on empiric vancomycin plus ceftriaxone. Three sets of blood cultures now grow penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci, and she has no drug allergies. Which directed therapy fits?","choices":["Vancomycin plus rifampin","Nafcillin or oxacillin","Penicillin G or ceftriaxone","Daptomycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy is narrowed once the organism is known. Penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci are treated with penicillin G or ceftriaxone, with or without gentamicin, so the directed choice is one of those drugs; empiric vancomycin was there to cover staphylococci until the organism was known. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 29"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with known aortic stenosis is seen in clinic with 3 weeks of low-grade fevers, night sweats, weight loss and a louder murmur. He is stable, and no antibiotics have been given. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Three culture sets from separate sites","One blood culture, then antibiotics","Start oral amoxicillin today","Antibiotics now, cultures later"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood cultures, three sets from separate sites, come first, and empiric therapy is started ONLY after they are drawn. Starting antibiotics before cultures is a recognized cause of culture-negative endocarditis, which would leave the organism unknown. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 25, 26"},{"q":"Four patients have infective endocarditis. Which one has the highest expected mortality?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus, native valve","Staphylococcus aureus, prosthetic valve","Viridans streptococci, treated early","Staphylococcus aureus, injects drugs"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the most lethal organism, and its mortality is highest with a prosthetic valve, higher than in people who do not inject drugs and much higher than in people who inject drugs. Viridans streptococci carry a relatively good prognosis when treated early. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with mitral regurgitation has 2 months of low-grade fever, and three sets of blood cultures grow Kingella kingae, a HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) organism. He has no drug allergies. Which antibiotic is first-line?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Vancomycin plus gentamicin","Nafcillin","A fluoroquinolone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) endocarditis is treated with ceftriaxone or another third-generation cephalosporin. With no allergy there is no reason to move off it. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 20, 31"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with fever, a new murmur and splinter hemorrhages (thin dark lines under the nails) has three sets of blood cultures drawn, and empiric antibiotics are about to start before any organism is known. Which organisms must the empiric regimen cover?","choices":["Enterococci and gram-negative rods","Staphylococci, streptococci, enterococci","Streptococci and HACEK only","Staphylococci and Candida"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy for endocarditis, started once blood cultures are drawn and before the organism is known, must cover staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci, which is why vancomycin is paired with ceftriaxone (or with gentamicin and cefepime for a prosthetic valve in its first year). — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 22, 27"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with previous infective endocarditis is scheduled for a dental cleaning at 9 AM. He has no allergies and will take oral amoxicillin. When should he take it?","choices":["The evening before","30-60 minutes before","Right after the cleaning","Daily for 3 days after"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prophylaxis is a single dose taken 30-60 minutes before the procedure, so the drug is already circulating when the cleaning releases bacteria into the blood. Previous endocarditis makes him high risk, and a cleaning manipulates the gums. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 35, 36"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy has a temperature of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F) and white-centered retinal hemorrhages (Roth spots) on fundoscopy. Blood cultures and echocardiography are pending. How many minor modified Duke criteria does he meet?","choices":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is on the list of predisposing heart conditions, fever of 38.9 °C is the fever criterion, and Roth spots are an immunologic phenomenon. Three minor criteria make the case possible while cultures and echocardiography are pending. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 12, 22"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with a bioprosthetic mitral valve has a temperature of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F), hemorrhages on both conjunctivae and a positive rheumatoid factor. One of three blood cultures grows Staphylococcus aureus; the other two show no growth. Transesophageal echocardiography shows no vegetation, abscess or dehiscence, and her murmur is unchanged. What is her modified Duke classification?","choices":["Possible: four minor criteria","Definite: one major, three minor","Possible: one major, one minor","Definite: five minor criteria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her prosthetic valve (predisposition), fever of 38.8 °C, conjunctival hemorrhages (vascular), rheumatoid factor (immunologic) and a single positive culture that does not meet the major criterion (microbiologic) are five minor criteria. Five minor criteria make endocarditis definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 23"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with rheumatic mitral regurgitation has 6 weeks of low-grade fever and weight loss. He has taken no antibiotics. Three sets of blood cultures drawn from separate sites show no growth, and transesophageal echocardiography shows a mitral vegetation. Which best explains the negative cultures?","choices":["Endocarditis has been excluded","Too few culture sets drawn","An organism routine cultures miss","Antibiotics given before cultures"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Culture-negative endocarditis may be due to organisms not recovered in routine microbiology testing or to antimicrobials given before blood cultures. He has taken no antibiotics and had three proper sets drawn, so an organism that routine testing does not recover is the explanation. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes has 5 weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue. Transesophageal echocardiography shows an aortic valve vegetation, and three sets of blood cultures grow penicillin-susceptible Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis). She has no drug allergies. Which antibiotic is appropriate?","choices":["Daptomycin","Nafcillin or oxacillin","A fluoroquinolone","Ceftriaxone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) is one of the other streptococci, treated with penicillin, cefazolin or ceftriaxone (or vancomycin plus ceftriaxone plus oral rifampin), and ceftriaxone is also listed for penicillin-susceptible Streptococcus gallolyticus. With a susceptible isolate and no allergy, ceftriaxone fits. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis has four procedures planned this year. Before which one should she receive prophylaxis?","choices":["Dental cleaning","Dental radiographs","Screening colonoscopy","Cystoscopy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Previous endocarditis makes her high risk, and prophylaxis is given for dental procedures that manipulate the gums or tooth roots, which includes dental cleaning. The other procedures are on the no-prophylaxis list. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 36"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man who uses intravenous drugs is admitted with 4 weeks of fever. An earlier echocardiogram showed a large aortic valve vegetation. Three blood cultures have grown Candida species, he is receiving fluconazole, and he suddenly develops a cold, blue toe. Which is the appropriate next step?","choices":["Surgery consult for aortic valve replacement","Switch fluconazole to amphotericin B","Repeat echo to see if the vegetation embolized","Aortic angiography for a mycotic aneurysm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Candida in three blood cultures with a large aortic vegetation is fungal endocarditis, and fungal infection is difficult to cure with medication alone, which makes it an indication for surgery. The cold, blue toe is the expected complication, embolism and occlusion of a larger peripheral artery, so the definitive step is cardiovascular surgery to replace the valve. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 4; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 33, 37"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with a transcatheter aortic valve needs a tooth extraction. His chart lists a penicillin allergy: years ago he had a mild, delayed rash with penicillin, with no hives, swelling or breathing trouble. He takes pills. Which agent fits?","choices":["Oral amoxicillin","Oral cephalexin","Oral clindamycin","Intravenous vancomycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A transcatheter-implanted prosthesis is a high-risk condition, and an extraction manipulates the gums. For a penicillin-allergic patient who can take pills, cephalexin is listed, and it is barred only after anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria, none of which he had. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 35, 36"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl had an atrial septal defect closed with a prosthetic device 3 years ago, and echocardiography shows no residual shunt. She needs a dental extraction. Which is appropriate regarding endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Prophylaxis only with a murmur","Give prophylaxis beforehand","No prophylaxis is needed","A 3-day course afterward"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Repaired congenital heart disease calls for prophylaxis only in the first 6 months after repair with prosthetic material or a device, or when a residual defect sits next to the prosthetic material. She is 3 years out with no residual shunt, so neither applies. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A dental surgeon is reviewing four adult patients booked for extractions. Which patient should receive endocarditis prophylaxis?","choices":["Coronary stents and prior bypass","Mitral prolapse without regurgitation","Heart transplant with valve regurgitation","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac transplant with valve regurgitation from a structurally abnormal valve is on the high-risk list, together with prosthetic valves or repair material, previous endocarditis and certain congenital heart disease. An extraction manipulates the gums, so this patient needs prophylaxis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 36"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman whose mitral valve was repaired with an annuloplasty ring needs a root canal. Years ago she developed lip and tongue swelling (angioedema) after ampicillin. She takes pills easily. Which prophylaxis fits?","choices":["Intramuscular cefazolin","Oral clarithromycin","No prophylaxis needed","Oral cephalexin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prosthetic material used for valve repair, such as an annuloplasty ring, is a high-risk condition, and a root canal manipulates the periapical region, so she needs prophylaxis. With a penicillin allergy and an oral route, clarithromycin or azithromycin (or doxycycline) fits; her angioedema bars cephalosporins. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 34, 35, 36"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman had her aortic valve replaced with a prosthesis 3 months ago. She asks when her risk of prosthetic valve endocarditis is highest. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Within the first year","From year 2 to year 5","After 10 years","The same at all times"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The greatest risk of prosthetic valve endocarditis is within the first year after replacement; it then gradually declines until it is similar to the risk for a native valve. At 3 months she is inside that window. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with type 2 diabetes is on day 3 of ampicillin plus gentamicin for Enterococcus faecalis endocarditis of his native aortic valve. His creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 2.6 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.2), an acute kidney injury, and his creatinine clearance has fallen. Which change is most appropriate?","choices":["Replace gentamicin with ceftriaxone","Continue ampicillin plus gentamicin","Switch to oral amoxicillin","Stop antibiotics until it recovers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone is the enterococcal combination marked as good for acute kidney injury or chronic kidney disease with a low creatinine clearance. Swapping gentamicin for ceftriaxone keeps a bactericidal two-drug regimen against the enterococcus while removing the drug kept away from failing kidneys. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 26, 30"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who injects drugs has a temperature of 39.0 °C (102.2 °F) and a cough, and chest imaging shows septic pulmonary infarcts. One of three blood cultures grows Staphylococcus aureus. Transthoracic and transesophageal echocardiography show no vegetation, abscess or new regurgitation. What is her modified Duke classification?","choices":["Possible: four minor criteria","Definite: one major, three minor","Definite: five minor criteria","Ruled out by the normal echo"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Injection drug use, fever, septic pulmonary infarcts (a vascular phenomenon) and a single positive culture that does not meet the major criterion are four minor criteria, with no major one. Three or more minor criteria make the case possible; definite needs five. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 25"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a mechanical aortic valve is being evaluated for endocarditis. She has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), and blood cultures are pending. Which finding would count as a major modified Duke criterion?","choices":["Painful red nodules on finger pads","Pinpoint conjunctival hemorrhages","Her prosthetic valve itself","New partial dehiscence of the valve"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Evidence of endocardial involvement is a major criterion, and new partial dehiscence of a prosthetic valve on echocardiography is one of its forms, along with an oscillating intracardiac mass and an abscess. The other findings are minor criteria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with no known heart disease has 2 weeks of fever to 38.8 °C (101.8 °F) that began after dental work, and pinpoint hemorrhages on both conjunctivae. One of three blood cultures grows viridans streptococci, and transesophageal echocardiography shows a vegetation on the mitral valve. What is her modified Duke classification?","choices":["Possible: one major, one minor","Definite: two major criteria","Definite: one major, three minor","Possible: one major, two minor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The mitral vegetation is a major criterion. Fever, conjunctival hemorrhages (vascular) and a single positive culture that does not meet the major criterion (microbiologic) are three minor criteria. One major plus three minor is definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 22, 24"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has native aortic valve endocarditis from viridans streptococci after a dental extraction. She had anaphylaxis to penicillin, and hives after ceftriaxone. Which antibiotic fits?","choices":["Ceftriaxone plus gentamicin","Penicillin G","Nafcillin","Vancomycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Viridans streptococci are treated with penicillin G or ceftriaxone (with or without gentamicin) or with vancomycin. Her anaphylaxis to penicillin and her hives after ceftriaxone rule out the first two, leaving vancomycin as the listed alternative. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is hospitalized with Streptococcus bovis (gallolyticus) endocarditis of the mitral valve. He recovers completely with appropriate therapy. Which is the most important next step?","choices":["Repeat echocardiography in 6 weeks","Mitral valve replacement to prevent emboli","Dental hygiene and denture fitting","Colonoscopy to look for mucosal lesions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Streptococcus gallolyticus (bovis) comes from the gastrointestinal tract and is associated with liver disease or cirrhosis and with gastrointestinal abnormalities, especially colon cancer. Once the endocarditis is cured, the most important step is looking for the colonic lesion that let the organism into the blood. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slides 1, 3; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 17, 33"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with a bioprosthetic aortic valve placed 6 years ago has subacute endocarditis, and three sets of blood cultures grow Cardiobacterium hominis. He had hives and throat swelling after amoxicillin, and hives again after a dose of ceftriaxone last year. Which antibiotic fits?","choices":["Ampicillin-sulbactam","Nafcillin or oxacillin","Ceftriaxone","A fluoroquinolone"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiobacterium hominis is a HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) organism, normally treated with ceftriaxone or another third-generation cephalosporin. He has reacted to both a penicillin and ceftriaxone, and for HACEK disease with a penicillin allergy a fluoroquinolone could be considered. — IE Missing Slide and Cases.pptx, Slide 1; 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 17, 31"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with normal kidney function has uncomplicated native mitral valve endocarditis from penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci. Her fever settled within 2 days of starting therapy, and she has no abscess or heart failure. The team wants the shortest course a regimen allows. Which regimen fits?","choices":["Ampicillin plus ceftriaxone","Vancomycin alone","Ceftriaxone alone","Ceftriaxone plus gentamicin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adding gentamicin to penicillin G or ceftriaxone allows a 2-week regimen for viridans streptococcal endocarditis, but only when it is uncomplicated, responds rapidly and there is no chronic kidney disease. She meets all three, so the combination gives the shortest course. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 27, 29, 30"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with mitral regurgitation has 2 months of low-grade fever, and blood cultures grow Eikenella corrodens, a gram-negative HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) organism. Where in the body do these organisms normally come from?","choices":["Genitourinary tract","Gastrointestinal tract","Upper respiratory tract","Skin surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) organisms are gram-negative bacteria whose source is the upper respiratory tract, and they are among the causes of subacute endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 17, 20"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a bioprosthetic aortic valve has a temperature of 38.6 °C (101.5 °F). One of four blood cultures grows coagulase-negative staphylococci, and the other three show no growth. Transesophageal echocardiography shows new partial dehiscence of the prosthesis. What is his modified Duke classification?","choices":["Definite: one major, three minor","Possible: one major, two minor","Possible: three minor criteria","Definite: two major criteria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. New partial dehiscence of a prosthetic valve is a major criterion. His prosthetic valve (predisposition) and fever are two minor criteria. A single culture of coagulase-negative staphylococci is excluded as a likely contaminant, so it adds nothing; one major plus two minor is possible. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy with congenital heart disease has infective endocarditis. His parents ask how children's endocarditis differs from adults'. Compared with adults, which feature is more typical in children?","choices":["Mostly degenerative valve disease","More peripheral skin signs","More often right-sided","More heart failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Compared with adults, pediatric endocarditis is more often right-sided, particularly with congenital heart disease, carries a higher septic embolism rate and causes less heart failure. Peripheral stigmata are less common. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slides 39, 41"}]};
